# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Arrays", worked example "Incrementing a cell".
id: array-increment
title: Incrementing a cell
topic: Arrays
level: 2
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Arrays, "Incrementing a cell"
tags: arrays, array assignment, select-store

task:
  Find the weakest precondition with the rule ARRAY-ASSIGN, following the
  three steps: substitution, select-store, and definedness.

pre: a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
post: a[i] > 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := a[i] + 1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Substitution
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: a[i] :=
text:
  The statement has the shape $a[I] := E$ with $I = i$ and
  $E = a[i] + 1$. We replace the array $a$ in the postcondition by the
  updated array $a[i |-> a[i] + 1]$. The $a$ inside $E$ is not replaced: it
  is the array before the write.
calc:
  (a[i] > 0)[a <- a[i |-> a[i] + 1]]
  = a[i |-> a[i] + 1][i] > 0

step: Select-store
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN, LOGIC
text:
  The read position is $i$ and the written position is also $i$. They are
  literally the same expression, so we know that $J = I$, and the first
  select-store axiom gives the written value.
calc:
  a[i |-> a[i] + 1][i] > 0
  = a[i] + 1 > 0
  = a[i] >= 0              // for integers

step: Definedness
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
text:
  The write is at $i$, and $E$ reads $a[i]$. Both give $def(a[i])$, which
  we write once.
calc:
  wp(a[i] := a[i] + 1, Q)
  = a[i |-> a[i] + 1][i] > 0 && def(a[i])
  = a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
set: R1 = a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
vc: a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) ==> R1
result: The weakest precondition is $a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)$.

summary:
  Test it with the array $4, 0, 9$ and $i = 1$: position $1$ exists and
  $a[1] = 0$ is not negative, so the precondition holds. The statement
  sets $a[1]$ to $1$, and $1 > 0$.
